# Database / Supabase

## Migrations are mandatory

ALL database schema changes MUST go through migration files. NO EXCEPTIONS.

- Location: `supabase/migrations/`
- Naming: `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_description.sql`
- Make migrations idempotent where possible (`IF NOT EXISTS`, `IF EXISTS`).
- Keep migrations small and focused on a single change.
- Comment the migration file to document schema decisions.

This covers: tables, columns, types, constraints, indexes, foreign keys, functions, triggers, views, RLS policies, and data migrations.

## Applying migrations

- The ONLY approved way to apply migrations to the remote Supabase project is `npx supabase db push`.
- NEVER apply schema changes via the Supabase Dashboard SQL Editor, MCP `execute_sql`, or a direct SQL client — these bypass migration tracking and cause sync issues.
- MCP `execute_sql` is for read-only inspection queries ONLY (never DDL).
- If the user explicitly requests a bypass method, warn about migration-tracking consequences and get confirmation first.
- Local development Postgres (Docker) may apply the same migration files directly with psql — the tracking discipline applies to the Supabase remote.

## Schema inspection

Prefer the Supabase MCP tools (`list_tables`, read-only `execute_sql`) for live schema context before falling back to reading migration files.

⚠️ Verify the MCP is connected to the Wild Rift Forge project before ANY write operation. A Supabase MCP connection may point at an unrelated production project.

## Conventions

- snake_case for tables, columns, and functions.
- Every table has `created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()`; mutable tables also have `updated_at`.
- History tables (`patch_changes`, `raw_sources`) are append-only — never overwrite historical patch data because current game data changed.
- Use JSONB for irregular structured metadata rather than forcing rigid columns.
- Keep the data model PostgreSQL-native; avoid proprietary Supabase features where standard Postgres works.
